Phase 1: Foundations & Legal Shield (Month 1)
You are building a company that handles high-stakes compliance. You must look like a "Firm," not a "Side Project."

Form the LLC: File the Articles of Organization with the Wisconsin DFI (WDFI.org).

Cost: $130 (Online).

Tip: Name it something professional like Reetz Cyber Automation LLC or Grafton Industrial Security.

EIN & Banking: Get your free EIN from the IRS and open a dedicated business bank account (e.g., Chase or a local credit union like Landmark). Never mix a single dollar of side income with your personal funds.

The Insurance Shield: Secure Technology Errors & Omissions (Tech E&O).

Target: A policy with $1M coverage. Given your background, expect to pay **$125–$200/mo**. This is non-negotiable before you touch a client's network.

Infrastructure: Buy a dedicated personal laptop and set up your Raspberry Pi lab. Ensure it never touches your work Wi-Fi or VPN.

Phase 2: The "Clean Room" Build (Month 2-3)
You are recreating the logic of your 130k-line library without using a single line of the employer's code.

The "NIST 20" Module: Use Antigravity Ultra to draft a fresh Python library. Focus strictly on the Top 20 NIST 800-171 controls that manufacturers fail most often (MFA, Logging, Asset Inventory).

Agentic Testing: Use one agent to write the scan code and a second "Audit Agent" to verify it against the public NIST 800-171 Rev 3 documentation.

The "One-Click" Report: Build a template in your library that takes raw scan data and generates a "C-Suite Ready" PDF. This is your primary product.

